This Rule was issued against the judgment and order dated 28.7.86 passed by the Subordinate Judge, Munshigonj dismissing the Misc. Appeal 382 of 1983, affirming the judgment and order dated 28.9.93 passed by the Munsif, 3rd Court, Munshigonj allowing the pre-emption Misc. Case 36 of 1980 under section 96 of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act (which shall hereinafter be called the Act) 

 &quot;Holding&#39; means a parcel or parcels of land or an undivided share thereof, held by a raiyat or an under-raiyat and forming the subject of a separate tenancy.&quot; 

 6. I have coddled the said decision and seen that the decision was made in respect of the homestead land falling within the Municipal area embracing within the ambit of Non-agricultural Tenancy Act, 1949. So, that decision has no application in the instant case. 

 &quot;agricultural land&#39; means land which is used for purposes connected with agriculture or horticulture and includes a homestead as defined in the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950 (EB Act XXVIII of 1951).&quot; 

 Mr. Karmaker, in support of his arguments, cites an unreported decision in the case of Tayeb Ali and another vs. Abdul Mutlib in Civil Appeal No. 30 of 1983 wherein their Lordships in Full Bench of our Appellate Division on 13.12.1983 accepted the said definition of agricultural land and held that homestead is included within the definition of agricultural land. Mr. Karmaker also cites the decision in Aseruddin Sk vs. Serajuddin Talukder, 30 DLR 75 wherein Mr. Justice Ranadhir Sen sitting singly decided that the homestead is included within agricultural holding and, as such, it is pre-emptible under section 96 of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act.
